  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: cedcb971d9918c84fb60186450419255, type: 3}
  m_Name: Module4
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::PhotoTextContentData
  data:
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display: []
    titleName: 
    description: "There are two types of storage devices used with computers: a primary
      storage device, such as RAM (random-access memory), and a secondary storage
      device, such as a hard disk drive (HDD) and solid-state drive (SSD). Secondary
      storage can be removable, internal, or external.\r\n \r\nRAM is usually associated
      with DRAM (Dynamic Random-Access Memory), a type of memory module. RAM is a
      volatile memory and requires power to keep the data accessible. If the computer
      is turned off, all data contained in RAM is lost.\r\nOver the evolution of
      the computer, there have been different variations of RAM. Today, as solid-state
      memory developed into (DRAM) then it became DDR, DDR2, DDR3, DDR4.\r\n"
    has3dView: 0
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: db02038ca6cc2f6439917b6e4ab43274, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: "Three basic types of RAM:\r\n1.     Dynamic RAM (DRAM) \u2013 This
      kind of RAM needs to be recharged by the CPU. If not, it loses its contents.
      There are many variations of DRAM such as:\n\no Synchronous DRAM (SDRAM) \u2013
      faster than DRAM because it is synchronized to the system clock\r\no Double
      Data Rate (DDR) SDRAM \u2013 faster than SDRAM because they transfer data twice
      for each clock cycle\r\no DDR2 and DDR3 SDRAM \u2013 faster than DDR SDRAM\r\no 
      ambus DRAM (RDRAM) \u2013 type of DRAM faster than SDRAM that uses pipelining
      techniques\r\n2.     Static RAM (SRAM) \u2013 This kind is faster and more
      reliable than any form of DRAM. Unlike the DRAM, this does not need to be re-energized.
      However, it is much more expensive and it used for special purposes.\r\n3.    
      Magnetoresistive RAM (MRAM) \u2013 This kind stores data using magnetic charges
      instead of electrical charges. It has greater storage capacity, consume less
      power, and faster access times.\r\n\r\n"
    has3dView: 1
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: edf67964a0dddef4eb88bc21559f1bcf, type: 3}
    titleName: The RAM chips are housed in a small circuit board called the memory
      module, which is inserted into the motherboard.
    description: "Three types of memory modules:\r\n1.     Single Inline Memory Module
      (SIMM) \u2013 this has pins on opposite sides of the circuit board which connect
      together to form a single set of contacts. This houses SDRAM chips along with
      DIMMs.\r\n2.     Dual Inline Memory Module (DIMM) \u2013 the pins on the opposite
      sides of the circuit board do not connect and form two sets of contacts. This
      houses SDRAM chips along with SIMMs.\r\n3.     Rambus Inline Memory Module
      (RIMM) \u2013 this houses RDRAM chips\r\n \r\n \r\nThe computer will need a
      certain amount of memory to be able to handle programs, data, and information,
      depending on the types of applications used. \r\nThe more RAM a computer has,
      the faster the computer will be. \r\n\xB7         To be able to use basic application
      software such as Office Suite, one needs at least 1GB of RAM. \r\n"
    has3dView: 1
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 6f4e10d26fd9ba04387f37ba4e6a035a, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: "  Using programs and multimedia should have at least 4GB. \r\n\xB7        
      Playing modern games or using editing software will require at least 8GB. \r\nToday
      the RAM ranges up to 64GB depending on the programs that will be used for the
      computer.\r\n"
    has3dView: 1
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: bdc6fb62dad2109488afdfeaeda90586, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: "Secondary Storage device is a non-volatile device that holds data
      until it is deleted or overwritten. A computer may not have to use secondary
      storage. However, the need to save the required data before the power turned
      off would be able to recover the last saved version of the file.\n\nExamples
      of secondary storage\r\n\xB7         Hard Disk Drive (HHD) \u2013 often called
      hard drive or hard disk. This is the most common storage device that do not
      need power to maintain data; these store data through spinning platters or
      magnetic disks, recording individual bits written into the disk.\r\n"
    has3dView: 1
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 64842c90d5cbd4343a4681fd1f32b94f, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: " Solid State Drive (SSD) \u2013 mass storage device that stores
      data in a permanent state even without power. Similar to an HDD\u2019s function,
      only the internal components are different. SSDs do not have moving parts but
      store data on magnetic platters making use of flash memory.\r\n"
    has3dView: 1
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 8e950ef1599779246a4e7d34ac4fc558, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: "  Flash Drive \u2013 highly portable small data storage devices
      making use of flash memory and a Universal Serial Bus (USB) port connection\r\n"
    has3dView: 1
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: fd3dcb7b274a04342a8b0f1634e4e040, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: '  SD (Secure Digital) card is a compact, removable flash memory
      card used to store and transfer digital data in portable electronic devices,
      such as digital cameras, smartphones, tablets, drones, and gaming consoles.'
    has3dView: 1
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: ef9ac5a1bf781854ebf98e1809a81f4c, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: "CD \u2013 \u201Ccompact disc\u201D, this storage device can hold
      up to 700 MB; it is read by an optical drive through a laser\r\n"
    has3dView: 1
  - contentName: 0
    modelName: 0
    display:
    - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 5ada54e0c942bbe4980248712b861cd2, type: 3}
    titleName: 
    description: 'DVD (digital versatile disc) this storage device can hold up to
      4.7 GB, while dual-layer discs can store up to 8.5 GB of data or video



      HDD
      vs. SSD: Key Differences

      a. HDDs (Hard Disk Drives) are traditional
      storage devices that use spinning disks and a moving read/write head to store
      and retrieve data.

      b. SSDs (Solid State Drives) use newer technology,
      storing data on memory chips that can be accessed instantly.

      Speed:
      SSDs are much faster.

      Noise: SSDs are quieter.

      Size and Durability:
      SSDs are smaller, more durable, and use less energy.

      Cost and Capacity:
      HDDs are cheaper and provide more storage space but are more prone to damage
      and have easier data recovery.

      Summary: HDDs are cost-effective for
      large storage, while SSDs offer better performance and durability.

'
    has3dView: 1
